/* CCAR-F Mock Exam 4 — 60 fresh items. Weighted toward the candidate's recurring misses across
   Mocks 1–3 while covering all 30 task statements. Contexts deliberately unlike earlier mocks
   (telecom billing, Android monorepo, dbt pipeline CI, insurance claims). D1x16 D2x11 D3x12 D4x12 D5x9.
   Community-sourced themes folded in: structural-over-prompt fixes, hard-coded-query subagent
   rigidity, unnecessary subagent spawning, batch SLA arithmetic, temporal-vs-conflict data. */
window.MOCK4 = {
  id: 'MOCK4',
  title: 'Mock Exam 4 — CCAR-F',
  minutes: 120,
  weights: { 1: .27, 2: .18, 3: .20, 4: .20, 5: .15 },
  scenarios: [
    { name: 'Customer Support Resolution Agent',
      blurb: 'You run a telecom operator’s billing-and-plan support agent on the Claude Agent SDK. It handles disputed charges, plan changes, SIM swaps and account holds through MCP tools (verify_subscriber, get_bill, apply_credit, change_plan, transfer_to_agent). Target: 80%+ first-contact resolution with correct escalation.' },
    { name: 'Developer Productivity with Claude',
      blurb: 'You are building productivity tooling for a large Android monorepo — Kotlin feature modules plus a legacy Java core — using the Agent SDK with the built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob) and MCP servers for Gradle, Jira and the artifact registry. Engineers use it to navigate unfamiliar modules, generate boilerplate and automate chores.' },
    { name: 'Claude Code for Continuous Integration',
      blurb: 'You are wiring Claude Code into CI for an analytics data-pipeline repository (dbt models, SQL, Python loaders). It reviews pipeline pull requests, proposes dbt tests, and posts feedback. Reviews must be actionable with few false positives; the team iterates on PRs frequently.' },
    { name: 'Structured Data Extraction',
      blurb: 'You are extracting structured claim records from unstructured motor-insurance intake: adjuster notes, repair-shop estimates, OCR’d scans and photo captions. Output is validated against JSON schemas and feeds an automated payout system, so accuracy and honest handling of missing or conflicting data matter.' }
  ],
  questions: [
    /* ===== S1 · Telecom support (D1×7, D2×4, D5×4) ===== */
    { scen:0, d:1, ts:'1.1', ans:[2],
      stem:'The agent often narrates its plan ("Let me pull up your bill first") in the same response that requests get_bill. Your loop exits the moment any text block appears, so the bill is never fetched. Which control flow is correct?',
      opts:['Exit only when the response contains no text at all, since narration means the model is still deciding.',
            'Exit after a fixed number of iterations, which bounds cost and covers most billing conversations.',
            'Continue while stop_reason is "tool_use" and exit when it is "end_turn", ignoring whether text is present.',
            'Exit when the narration contains a phrase like "here is your answer", which signals completion.'],
      why:'A single turn can carry both narration and a tool_use block. stop_reason is the only reliable completion signal: "tool_use" means run the tools and loop; "end_turn" means present the answer. Text presence and phrase-matching are documented anti-patterns; a fixed cap is a cost safety net, not a completion signal.' },
    { scen:0, d:1, ts:'1.1', ans:[0],
      stem:'In one turn the model requests both verify_subscriber and get_bill. Your code executes only the first and sends its result; the API rejects the follow-up request. What was required?',
      opts:['One user message containing a tool_result for each requested call, each paired by tool_use_id.',
            'Two consecutive user messages, one per tool result, sent in the order the tools were requested.',
            'A single tool_result whose content concatenates both outputs, since the turn is treated as one call.',
            'Only the first result, with the second call re-issued by the model on the following turn.'],
      why:'Parallel tool calls are answered together: every tool_use in the turn gets its own tool_result, all inside one user message, matched by id. Leaving a call unanswered is rejected by the API.' },
    { scen:0, d:1, ts:'1.4', ans:[1],
      stem:'A SIM swap must never be issued without identity verification — fraud exposure is severe. The system prompt states this in capitals, yet 3% of swaps still proceed on the caller’s word alone. What is the correct enforcement?',
      opts:['Move the rule to the first line of the system prompt and repeat it in every user turn.',
            'Add a programmatic prerequisite that blocks change_plan/SIM-swap calls until verify_subscriber has returned a verified subscriber id.',
            'Provide several few-shot transcripts showing verification happening before every swap.',
            'Lower the sampling temperature so the model applies the verification rule consistently.'],
      why:'A required sequence guarding a high-consequence action is an invariant. Prompt emphasis, examples and temperature all leave a non-zero failure rate; a prerequisite gate makes the violating call impossible. Structural beats probabilistic when the failure is fraud.' },
    { scen:0, d:1, ts:'1.5', ans:[0],
      stem:'get_bill returns amounts in paise as integers, apply_credit expects rupees as a decimal string, and the legacy plan service returns formatted strings like "₹1,240.50". The agent occasionally applies a credit 100× too large. Where does the fix belong?',
      opts:['A PostToolUse hook that normalises every tool’s money values to one representation before the model reads them.',
            'A system-prompt table documenting each tool’s money format and instructing careful conversion.',
            'A PreToolUse hook that rejects any tool call whose arguments might use an unexpected money format.',
            'A dedicated convert_currency tool the agent is told to call after each billing lookup.'],
      why:'Unit conversion is deterministic and must be right every time, so it belongs in code that runs every time. PostToolUse fires after a result returns and rewrites it, so only one format ever reaches the model. PreToolUse runs before execution — there is no result to normalise yet. Prompt tables fail occasionally, forever.' },
    { scen:0, d:1, ts:'1.5', ans:[0],
      stem:'Credits above ₹2,000 require a supervisor. You want the agent physically unable to apply larger credits and to route those cases to transfer_to_agent instead. Which hook design?',
      opts:['A PreToolUse hook on apply_credit that denies amounts above ₹2,000 with a reason naming the transfer workflow.',
            'A PostToolUse hook on apply_credit that reverses any credit above ₹2,000 after it has been applied.',
            'A Stop hook that reviews the finished conversation and flags credits above ₹2,000 for audit.',
            'A SessionStart hook that loads the ₹2,000 policy into the model’s context at the beginning of each session.'],
      why:'PreToolUse intercepts the outgoing call and can deny it before execution, and the denial reason steers the model to the alternative workflow. PostToolUse is too late — the credit already posted. Stop and SessionStart fire at the wrong lifecycle points and cannot block anything.' },
    { scen:0, d:1, ts:'1.4', ans:[2],
      stem:'When the agent transfers a disputed-bill case, its handoff reads "customer unhappy about charges". Human agents then redo the whole investigation. What should the handoff carry?',
      opts:['The full conversation transcript, so the human can form an independent view of the dispute.',
            'The customer’s tone and urgency rating, so the human can prioritise their queue correctly.',
            'Subscriber id, the disputed line items with amounts and dates, steps already taken, and a recommended credit.',
            'The raw responses from every tool the agent called, so nothing the agent saw is lost.'],
      why:'The receiving human lacks the transcript, so a structured handoff transfers case state — identity, findings, figures, recommendation — and the customer does not start over. Raw tool output preserves information but not conclusions; tone conveys mood, not the case.' },
    { scen:0, d:1, ts:'1.2', ans:[0,2],
      stem:'To "save a hop", an engineer proposes letting the bill-lookup subagent message the credits subagent directly instead of returning to the coordinator. Which TWO reasons explain why hub-and-spoke routing should be kept? (Select 2.)',
      opts:['The coordinator is the single place to observe what was delegated, what returned, and what failed.',
            'Subagents share one context window, so direct messaging would desynchronise their state.',
            'One error-handling policy applies at the coordinator instead of whichever peer happened to receive a failure.',
            'Direct messaging between subagents is not technically possible in the Agent SDK.'],
      why:'Hub-and-spoke is a design choice for observability, consistent error handling and controlled information flow. Subagents never share a context window, and the reason is not a technical impossibility — it is that the coordinator should decide what each agent sees.' },
    { scen:0, d:2, ts:'2.1', ans:[1],
      stem:'verify_subscriber and get_bill are both described as "Looks up account information". Requests about last month’s charges frequently trigger verify_subscriber. What is the best first step?',
      opts:['Add a dozen few-shot examples routing charge questions to get_bill.',
            'Rewrite both descriptions with input formats, example queries, edge cases, and explicit when-NOT-to-use boundaries.',
            'Insert a keyword router that inspects each message and pre-selects the tool before the model runs.',
            'Merge the two into one account_lookup tool that decides internally which backend to query.'],
      why:'Descriptions are the primary selection mechanism and these two are identical. Rewriting them acts on the root cause at no cost — the right first step. A router bypasses the model’s understanding; merging is heavier than a first step warrants; few-shot adds tokens without fixing the description gap.' },
    { scen:0, d:2, ts:'2.2', ans:[0],
      stem:'apply_credit rejects a request because the credit would exceed the plan’s monthly credit cap. What should the tool return so the agent handles it well?',
      opts:['isError true, errorCategory "business", isRetryable false, and a customer-friendly explanation of the cap.',
            'isError true, errorCategory "transient", isRetryable true, so the agent retries in case the cap resets.',
            'A normal success result with an empty credit object, letting the agent infer the credit was not applied.',
            'The policy engine’s full rejection payload with internal rule identifiers so no detail is lost.'],
      why:'A cap is policy: a business error, never retryable. isRetryable false stops wasted retries, and customer-friendly wording lets the agent relay the refusal and offer alternatives. Empty-as-success is silent suppression; internal rule ids are noise the agent cannot use.' },
    { scen:0, d:2, ts:'2.2', ans:[0,1],
      stem:'get_bill sometimes fails to locate a bill and returns the sentence "Unable to locate bill for this cycle" as an ordinary text result. The agent reads it aloud to customers as if it were bill content. Which TWO changes make the failure recognisable? (Select 2.)',
      opts:['Set the MCP isError flag on the result so the failure is a protocol-level condition, not prose.',
            'Put a structured error body (category, retryable, message) inside the result content.',
            'Prefix the text with "ERROR:" so the model is more likely to notice it.',
            'Throw an unhandled exception so the MCP connection drops and the agent retries.'],
      why:'Two layers: the envelope must say isError so the agent knows this is a failure, and the payload should be structured so it knows what kind. Structure alone inside a "successful" result still reads as content; a prefix relies on prose conventions; dropping the connection turns a recoverable error into an outage.' },
    { scen:0, d:2, ts:'2.3', ans:[1],
      stem:'The support agent was given all 19 operator tools "in case", including network diagnostics and admin utilities, and now misfires on tool selection. What does the blueprint prescribe?',
      opts:['Keep all 19 but list the billing tools first so they receive selection preference.',
            'Restrict the agent to the four or five tools its support role actually needs.',
            'Wrap the 19 behind a single dispatch tool that takes an action name.',
            'Leave it — tools the agent never calls have no effect on its behaviour.'],
      why:'Every tool present is a candidate answer to every selection; 19 versus 4–5 is a reliability difference by itself, and out-of-role tools get misused because they are there. Ordering is not a contract; a dispatcher hides the same wide choice in a parameter.' },
    { scen:0, d:5, ts:'5.2', ans:[0],
      stem:'A subscriber’s first message: "Put me through to a person. I am not doing this with a chatbot." The problem is a plan name misprinted on the bill — a ten-second fix. What should the agent do?',
      opts:['Transfer immediately, without looking anything up — an explicit request for a human is honoured as stated.',
            'Pull the bill first so the human receives a complete handoff, then transfer.',
            'Explain that the fix takes ten seconds and offer to do it before transferring.',
            'Apply a sentiment check and transfer only if frustration exceeds the threshold.'],
      why:'An explicit request for a human is a first-class trigger and unconditional — the ease of the issue is irrelevant. Even a lookup "for the handoff" continues working a case the customer closed to the agent. The human can gather details themselves.' },
    { scen:0, d:5, ts:'5.2', ans:[2],
      stem:'A subscriber writes: "Fourth call this month, you people are useless." The underlying issue is a duplicated ₹499 charge the agent can reverse within policy. What should it do?',
      opts:['Transfer to a human immediately — four calls and open hostility signal a case beyond the agent.',
            'Run sentiment analysis and transfer automatically because negative sentiment is extreme.',
            'Acknowledge the frustration, reverse the duplicate charge, and transfer only if they then ask for a person.',
            'Ask the subscriber to confirm they do not want a human before touching the charge.'],
      why:'Anger is not a complexity signal and not an escalation trigger. The case is within policy and capability, so resolve it warmly; escalate only on an explicit request for a human or a genuine policy gap. Transferring on hostility throws away first-contact resolution.' },
    { scen:0, d:5, ts:'5.1', ans:[0],
      stem:'Each get_bill call returns more than 50 fields — tower ids, tax codes, roaming flags — and a dispute session can make eight or nine calls. By the end the agent misquotes the two disputed amounts. Which change does the blueprint prescribe?',
      opts:['Trim each bill result to the dispute-relevant fields before it enters context, and hold the disputed amounts and dates in a persistent case-facts block.',
            'Cache the full bill objects outside the conversation and let the agent query the cache whenever it needs a figure.',
            'Ask get_bill only once at the start of the session and instruct the model to remember every field it returned.',
            'Move to a larger-context model so all nine full bill objects remain in the window without compression.'],
      why:'Tool results consume tokens far out of proportion to their relevance; trim at the source so junk never accumulates, and extract the facts that must survive into a block outside any summarisation. A bigger window is the capacity fallacy — dilution degrades recall even when everything fits.' },
    { scen:0, d:5, ts:'5.4', ans:[0],
      stem:'A 3 a.m. deploy killed the overnight reconciliation run halfway; on restart every subagent re-analysed all 40,000 bills from scratch. Which design would have let the run pick up where it stopped?',
      opts:['Have each subagent write its progress and findings to a known state file as it works, so the coordinator can load that manifest on restart and seed the agents with it.',
            'Have the coordinator save its complete transcript after every turn and reload it verbatim so the agents resume with identical context.',
            'Have each subagent keep a detailed log so an operator can reassign whatever work remained after the interruption.',
            'Record every subagent’s session id and resume each session after the restart so its prior context returns.'],
      why:'Structured state persistence: agents export curated state to a known location during the run; recovery is the coordinator loading that manifest and injecting it into fresh prompts. Replaying transcripts is bulky and stale-prone, sessions restore conversation rather than designed state, and operator reassignment is manual rework.' },
    { scen:1, d:2, ts:'2.5', ans:[0],
      stem:'LegacyUserStore must become AccountRepository at all 14 mentions inside one Kotlin file. Most efficient built-in approach?',
      opts:['Edit with replace_all set to true, old string LegacyUserStore, new string AccountRepository.',
            'Read the file, then Write it back with the fourteen mentions changed by hand.',
            'Bash with sed to perform a global substitution across the file.',
            'Fourteen Edit calls, each anchored with enough surrounding context to be unique.'],
      why:'Same change at every occurrence in one file is exactly replace_all: one call, diff only. Read+Write is the recovery path for a different case (a single change with a non-unique anchor); sed bypasses the built-in tool’s safety and is the standing distractor.' },
    { scen:1, d:2, ts:'2.5', ans:[0],
      stem:'Edit fails with a non-unique match because retryCount = 3 appears in four build-config blocks, and you need to change only the one under the release flavour. Correct next step?',
      opts:['Read the whole file, then Write it back with only the release block changed.',
            'Retry Edit with replace_all so the match is no longer ambiguous.',
            'Use Bash with sed, addressing the release block by its line number.',
            'Run Grep to find the line, then re-issue the identical Edit.'],
      why:'A single intended change with a non-unique anchor → Read + Write, the documented fallback. replace_all would change all four blocks; sed by line number is brittle and not the built-in path; Grep locates but does not make the anchor unique.' },
    { scen:1, d:2, ts:'2.5', ans:[1],
      stem:'You need every place fetchProfile( is called, and separately a list of all files named *Test.kt. Which tool serves each?',
      opts:['Glob for the call sites, because call sites follow naming patterns; Grep for test files, which mention "Test".',
            'Grep for the call sites, a content search; Glob for the test files, a path pattern.',
            'Grep for both — one search for fetchProfile( and another for files whose text mentions tests.',
            'Read candidate files for call sites; Glob for the test files by name.'],
      why:'Grep searches contents — a call site is text inside files. Glob matches paths — *Test.kt finds files by name regardless of directory. Content question → Grep; naming question → Glob.' },
    { scen:1, d:2, ts:'2.4', ans:[0],
      stem:'The team’s Gradle MCP server must reach every engineer on clone, but it needs each engineer’s personal artifact-registry token. How is it configured?',
      opts:['In project-scoped .mcp.json with ${REGISTRY_TOKEN} expansion, resolved from each engineer’s environment.',
            'In each engineer’s user-scoped ~/.claude.json, with setup steps documented in the README.',
            'In project-scoped .mcp.json with the token inline, since the repository is private.',
            'In user-scoped ~/.claude.json with ${REGISTRY_TOKEN} expansion, committed to the repo.'],
      why:'Shared tooling belongs in the committed .mcp.json; env-var expansion keeps the credential out of the commit and lets each machine supply its own. ~/.claude.json is never committed; an inline token in a repo is a leaked secret regardless of visibility.' },
    { scen:1, d:2, ts:'2.4', ans:[0],
      stem:'Agents burn several turns calling list_modules and list_flavors just to learn what exists in the monorepo before doing real work. What is the MCP-native fix?',
      opts:['Expose the module map and build flavours as MCP resources, so agents see what exists without tool calls.',
            'Add one list_everything tool that returns the whole map in a single invocation.',
            'Paste the module map into the coordinator’s system prompt so discovery costs nothing.',
            'Enable list_changed notifications so the server pushes module updates to the agent.'],
      why:'Resources exist to expose content catalogs for reference without exploratory tool calls. A list tool still spends a round trip and selection budget; pasting the map bloats every request; list_changed is about server-side updates, not discovery.' },
    { scen:1, d:2, ts:'2.1', ans:[0],
      stem:'Tool descriptions are excellent, yet any request containing the word "build" — including "build a mental model of this module" — routes to run_gradle_build. What should you inspect?',
      opts:['The system prompt, for keyword-sensitive instructions that echo the tool name (for example "always run the build before…").',
            'The MCP server manifest, for a priority ordering that ranks run_gradle_build first.',
            'The input schemas, for a required field named "build" shared between tools.',
            'The sampling temperature, which is causing overly literal keyword matching.'],
      why:'System-prompt wording steers selection: an instruction repeating a word from a tool name builds an association that overrides good descriptions. Priority orderings and manifest rankings do not exist as selection mechanisms.' },
    { scen:1, d:2, ts:'2.3', ans:[0],
      stem:'Every engineering request must first pass through classify_issue before any other tool is considered. Which configuration guarantees that?',
      opts:['tool_choice {"type":"tool","name":"classify_issue"} on the first turn, relaxing to auto or any afterwards.',
            'tool_choice "any", since the model will naturally begin with classification.',
            'tool_choice "auto" with a system-prompt rule that classification always comes first.',
            'Listing classify_issue first in the tools array, since earlier tools are preferred.'],
      why:'Forced selection is the only setting that guarantees a specific tool runs; it applies to that request, and later turns relax. "any" guarantees some tool, not which; "auto" plus a rule is a probabilistic request; list order is not a contract.' },
    { scen:1, d:3, ts:'3.1', ans:[0,1],
      stem:'The team split its 600-line CLAUDE.md into @docs/kotlin-style.md, @docs/testing.md and @docs/gradle.md, expecting a smaller context footprint. Context usage did not change. Which TWO statements about @import are accurate? (Select 2.)',
      opts:['Imported files expand at launch, so imports organise content but do not reduce what enters context.',
            'Import paths resolve relative to the file that contains the import.',
            'Imported files load lazily on first reference, reducing tokens versus inline text.',
            'Imports may nest to any depth because each file expands before its own imports are read.'],
      why:'@import is an organisation tool, not a context saver — files expand at launch alongside the importing file, and paths resolve relative to that file. Nesting caps at four hops. To load less, use path-scoped rules.' },
    { scen:1, d:3, ts:'3.3', ans:[0],
      stem:'Compose UI conventions apply only to files under feature/*/ui/**/*.kt, spread across 30 feature modules. Most maintainable configuration?',
      opts:['One .claude/rules/ file with paths frontmatter matching feature/*/ui/**/*.kt, loading when a matching file is read.',
            'A CLAUDE.md in each of the 30 feature modules containing a copy of the UI conventions.',
            'A section in the root CLAUDE.md, trusting Claude to notice when a file is UI code.',
            'A skill engineers invoke by hand whenever they are about to write Compose code.'],
      why:'A convention following a file pattern spread across the tree is the glob-rule case: one paths pattern covers every match wherever it lives and loads only when relevant. Per-module CLAUDE.md means 30 copies to keep in sync; a skill needs manual invocation.' },
    { scen:1, d:3, ts:'3.2', ans:[0],
      stem:'A /map-module skill dumps roughly 800 lines of Gradle dependency output into the main conversation, crowding out the task that invoked it. Which frontmatter fixes this?',
      opts:['context: fork — the skill runs in an isolated subagent context and returns only its summary.',
            'disable-model-invocation: true — the skill then runs only when an engineer types it.',
            'allowed-tools: Read — restricting tools reduces how much the skill emits.',
            'argument-hint: "[module]" — narrowing the analysis bounds its output.'],
      why:'context: fork is packaged context isolation: the verbose exploration stays in the fork. disable-model-invocation controls when the skill runs, not where its output lands; tool restriction and argument hints change what it does, not where 800 lines go.' },
    { scen:1, d:3, ts:'3.4', ans:[0],
      stem:'Which of these tasks belongs in plan mode rather than direct execution?',
      opts:['Migrating dependency injection from Dagger to Hilt across roughly 60 modules with several viable sequencing strategies.',
            'Fixing a NullPointerException in one ViewModel where the stack trace names the exact line.',
            'Adding a debug log line to a single repository class.',
            'Renaming a private variable inside one function.'],
      why:'Plan mode earns its cost on architectural, multi-file, multi-approach work — exactly a 60-module DI migration. The other three are well-scoped single changes where planning is overhead.' },
    { scen:1, d:1, ts:'1.6', ans:[0],
      stem:'"Move every module from Java 8 to Java 17" — the team does not know how library upgrades will cascade between modules. Which decomposition?',
      opts:['Adaptive: map the dependency graph first, rank modules by blast radius, and keep a plan that updates as breakages are discovered.',
            'A fixed chain: list modules alphabetically and upgrade each in turn until the list is exhausted.',
            'Parallel fan-out: one subagent per module, each upgrading its module independently.',
            'Direct execution starting at the app module, letting compile errors reveal the order.'],
      why:'When the next step depends on what the last step discovers, subtasks cannot be enumerated up front — that is the adaptive case. Fixed and parallel-by-module plans organise around what is already listable while the risk lives in the unknown dependency interactions.' },
    { scen:1, d:1, ts:'1.6', ans:[1],
      stem:'A 22-file change must be reviewed for style, then API compatibility, then test coverage — three known, independent aspects. Which decomposition?',
      opts:['Adaptive decomposition, deriving which aspects matter from each file’s content.',
            'Prompt chaining: a fixed sequence of focused passes, one per aspect.',
            'A single pass instructing the model to check all three aspects thoroughly.',
            'One subagent that decides at runtime which aspects are worth checking.'],
      why:'If you can list the steps before starting, use a fixed chain — predictable, testable, and it avoids the attention dilution of one combined pass. Adaptive decomposition is for open-ended work where the next step depends on findings.' },
    { scen:1, d:1, ts:'1.7', ans:[0],
      stem:'You want to resume a two-day-old analysis session. Since then only NetworkModule.kt and di/AppGraph.kt have changed; everything else the session read is unchanged. Most efficient correct approach?',
      opts:['Resume the session and tell Claude exactly those two files changed, so it re-reads only them.',
            'Start a fresh session and inject a structured summary of the earlier conclusions.',
            'Resume as-is; the agent will re-read any file it needs before relying on it.',
            'Resume and run /compact first so stale results are compressed away.'],
      why:'Magnitude decides. Two known files changed is minor drift: resume (keeping the expensive analysis) and name the files for targeted re-reading. Fresh-plus-summary is for when most of the analysis is stale; resuming blind trusts stale content; /compact compresses stale facts without correcting them.' },
    { scen:1, d:1, ts:'1.7', ans:[0],
      stem:'From one completed dependency-graph analysis, you want to explore a Hilt migration and a Koin migration independently, keeping the ability to return to either. What do you use?',
      opts:['Fork the analysed session twice, giving two independent branches from the same baseline.',
            'Resume the analysed session twice in sequence, asking for one migration then the other.',
            'Start two fresh sessions and paste a written summary of the analysis into each.',
            'Explore both migrations inside the original session so the model can compare them.'],
      why:'Forking seeds a new session with a copy of the baseline history and leaves the original intact; each fork has its own id. Sequential resumes contaminate the second exploration with the first; one session blurs the two.' },

    /* ===== S5 · CI for a dbt data pipeline (D3×8, D4×7) ===== */
    { scen:2, d:3, ts:'3.6', ans:[0],
      stem:'A GitHub Actions step runs claude "Review this dbt pull request" and the job stalls until it times out, with logs showing Claude Code awaiting input. Fix?',
      opts:['Add the -p flag: claude -p "Review this dbt pull request".',
            'Set CLAUDE_HEADLESS=true in the job’s environment.',
            'Redirect stdin from /dev/null so nothing can block.',
            'Add --batch so Claude Code queues the review for offline processing.'],
      why:'-p (--print) is the non-interactive mode: run the prompt, print to stdout, exit. CLAUDE_HEADLESS and --batch do not exist; the stdin redirect is a Unix workaround that does not change how Claude Code decides to run interactively.' },
    { scen:2, d:3, ts:'3.6', ans:[0],
      stem:'Review findings must be posted as inline PR comments by a script, so they need a fixed machine-readable shape. Which invocation?',
      opts:['claude -p "…" --output-format json --json-schema findings.schema.json',
            'claude -p "…" with a prompt asking for CSV-formatted output.',
            'claude -p "…" and parse the markdown headings in the reply.',
            'claude -p "…" --output-format json with no schema, trusting the model’s field names.'],
      why:'--output-format json makes output machine-parseable; --json-schema constrains it to your shape, so the posting script never guesses field names. Asking for CSV or parsing markdown is fragile prose-parsing.' },
    { scen:2, d:3, ts:'3.6', ans:[0],
      stem:'Pipeline PRs iterate many times, and the review bot re-posts identical comments on every push, so developers have started ignoring it. Fix?',
      opts:['Include the previous review’s findings in context and instruct Claude to report only new or still-unaddressed issues.',
            'Deduplicate comments in the pipeline script by exact string match against earlier ones.',
            'Post review output only on the first push of each PR and suppress later passes.',
            'Delete all prior bot comments before each run so only the newest remain.'],
      why:'Give the model the prior findings and ask for the delta — "addressed" is a semantic judgement string-matching cannot make. First-run-only goes blind to issues introduced later; deleting comments loses history.' },
    { scen:2, d:3, ts:'3.6', ans:[0],
      stem:'Generated dbt tests keep duplicating scenarios the repository already covers. What is the fix?',
      opts:['Provide the existing test files in context and ask for tests covering what is missing.',
            'Raise the temperature so the generator produces more novel test ideas.',
            'Ask the prompt for "unusual" tests only, to steer away from common cases.',
            'Generate freely and have a developer diff the output against existing tests.'],
      why:'The generator duplicates because it cannot see what exists. Show it the current tests and ask for gaps. Temperature and adjectives do not supply the missing information; manual diffing shifts the work to humans.' },
    { scen:2, d:3, ts:'3.6', ans:[0],
      stem:'The same Claude session that wrote a SQL migration is then asked to review it and misses a bug in its own logic. Why does an independent instance do better?',
      opts:['The generating session retains the reasoning that produced the bug, so it is unlikely to question its own decisions.',
            'The generating session has too little context left after writing the migration to review thoroughly.',
            'Review and generation instructions conflict within one session, blending the two roles.',
            'An independent instance can use a stronger model, and review benefits more from capability.'],
      why:'The contamination is the retained context: the assumptions that produced the bug are still present and defend it. A fresh instance sees only the code. Self-review instructions and extended thinking do not remove context — separation does.' },
    { scen:2, d:3, ts:'3.5', ans:[0],
      stem:'You have described a SQL date-bucketing transformation in prose three times, and each attempt buckets differently. Which refinement technique applies?',
      opts:['Provide two or three concrete input → expected-output example pairs for the transformation.',
            'Rewrite the prose with more precise terminology and numbered sub-steps.',
            'Ask Claude to restate the requirement in its own words before trying again.',
            'Split the transformation into smaller CTEs and request each separately.'],
      why:'When natural language is interpreted inconsistently, concrete examples are the named fix — they carry the specification unambiguously, including edge cases prose omits. A fourth redescription fails like the first three.' },
    { scen:2, d:3, ts:'3.5', ans:[0],
      stem:'Claude’s output has two bugs in the same window function that interact — fixing either changes the other — plus an unrelated typo in the README. How do you sequence the fixes?',
      opts:['Describe both window-function bugs in one message; handle the README typo separately.',
            'Fix all three in one comprehensive message so no context is lost.',
            'Fix each of the three sequentially, verifying after each.',
            'Fix the README typo first as the quick win, then the two bugs one at a time.'],
      why:'Interacting problems go in one message so the fix accounts for both; independent problems are handled separately. The two bugs interact; the typo does not.' },
    { scen:2, d:3, ts:'3.1', ans:[0],
      stem:'CI-invoked Claude proposes low-value dbt tests and misses the team’s fixture conventions. Where should testing standards and fixture guidance live so CI runs pick them up?',
      opts:['In the project CLAUDE.md committed to the repository, which CI-invoked Claude Code loads.',
            'In a user-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md on the CI runner’s home directory.',
            'Appended to the prompt string of every CI invocation.',
            'In a skill that a developer invokes manually before merging.'],
      why:'CLAUDE.md is the project context channel and the CI instance reads it from the repo. A user-level file on the runner is invisible to developers and not versioned; per-invocation prompts duplicate content; a manual skill is not automatic.' },
    { scen:2, d:4, ts:'4.1', ans:[0],
      stem:'The review bot floods dbt PRs with naming and formatting nitpicks. Adding "be conservative, report only high-confidence issues" did nothing. What works?',
      opts:['Define categories to report (correctness, data-loss risk) and categories to skip (formatting, naming).',
            'Move the conservatism instruction to the first line of the system prompt.',
            'Repeat the conservatism instruction before each file the bot analyses.',
            'Lower the temperature so borderline findings are suppressed.'],
      why:'"Conservative" and "high-confidence" are thresholds without a scale — they change tone, not the boundary. Categorical report/skip criteria turn self-grading into classification, which models do reliably.' },
    { scen:2, d:4, ts:'4.1', ans:[0],
      stem:'"SQL style" findings are 68% false positives, and developers have begun dismissing data-loss findings too. What is the right operational move?',
      opts:['Temporarily disable the style category while its prompt is improved, protecting trust in the accurate categories.',
            'Keep every category live and add a banner asking developers for patience while precision improves.',
            'Rename "SQL style" to "SQL quality" so the findings read as more substantive.',
            'Downgrade style findings to informational severity so they are visually de-emphasised.'],
      why:'A high-false-positive category is contagious — it teaches developers to ignore the whole bot. Disabling it protects the credibility of the accurate categories while it is fixed offline. A banner asks humans to absorb a cost the configuration should eliminate.' },
    { scen:2, d:4, ts:'4.1', ans:[0],
      stem:'Severity labels on findings are inconsistent — the same class of issue is "critical" in one PR and "minor" in the next. What fixes classification?',
      opts:['Define each severity level with a concrete code example so the scale is anchored.',
            'Ask the model to attach a numeric confidence score to each severity assignment.',
            'Add more descriptive adjectives to each severity level’s definition.',
            'Let developers re-grade severities and feed the corrections back weekly.'],
      why:'Worked examples anchor the scale; adjectives and self-reported confidence do not. Developer re-grading is a feedback loop but does not fix the prompt that produced the inconsistency.' },
    { scen:2, d:4, ts:'4.2', ans:[0],
      stem:'The bot repeatedly flags a legitimate incremental-model pattern as a correctness bug. Which technique reduces the false positives while keeping genuine bug detection?',
      opts:['Few-shot examples contrasting the acceptable incremental pattern with genuine issues, with reasoning for each.',
            'A blanket instruction never to flag anything that resembles the incremental pattern.',
            'Permanently disabling the correctness category for incremental models.',
            'Lowering the temperature so borderline findings are dropped.'],
      why:'Contrasting examples with reasoning let the model generalise the boundary between acceptable and problematic. A blanket ban also suppresses real bugs in that shape; disabling the category loses it entirely.' },
    { scen:2, d:4, ts:'4.6', ans:[0],
      stem:'A 19-file pipeline PR reviewed in one pass gets deep comments on some models, shallow on others, and contradictory verdicts on identical SQL in two files. Restructure how?',
      opts:['Per-file passes for local issues, then a separate integration pass for cross-model data flow.',
            'Move to a larger-context model so all 19 files receive attention in one pass.',
            'Require developers to split pipeline PRs into batches of three or four models.',
            'Run three independent full-PR passes and keep only findings that appear in at least two.'],
      why:'The cause is attention dilution, not capacity — the files already fit. Per-file passes give consistent depth; an integration pass catches cross-file issues. Splitting PRs shifts burden to developers; consensus voting suppresses real single-run findings.' },
    { scen:2, d:4, ts:'4.5', ans:[1],
      stem:'A nightly technical-debt report has a 36-hour turnaround SLA and will use the Message Batches API, whose processing can take up to 24 hours. What is the longest submission interval that still guarantees the SLA?',
      opts:['24 hours, since that matches the maximum batch processing time.',
            '12 hours, since interval plus 24-hour worst-case processing must not exceed 36 hours.',
            '36 hours, since the SLA itself is 36 hours.',
            '6 hours, to leave a generous safety margin below the SLA.'],
      why:'Worst case is the submission interval plus 24 hours of processing. 12 + 24 = 36 exactly meets the SLA; anything longer can breach it, and shorter intervals meet it but cost more submissions. The longest compliant interval is the cost-optimal one.' },
    { scen:2, d:4, ts:'4.5', ans:[0,1],
      stem:'An analytics lead makes five claims about the Message Batches API while planning cost cuts. Which TWO are correct? (Select 2.)',
      opts:['A pre-merge check that blocks a developer from merging should not be moved onto batches.',
            'Weekly schema-drift audits and nightly test suggestions are a good fit for batching.',
            'Off-peak batches finish inside an hour, so they are safe even for gating workflows.',
            'A batched request can call a tool, receive the result, and carry on within that same request.',
            'Responses come back in the order the requests were submitted, so no correlation id is needed.'],
      why:'Blocking work stays synchronous; latency-tolerant work takes the 50% discount. The other three are false: there is no completion-time guarantee at any hour, a batch cannot execute a tool mid-request (one request, one response), and results are matched by custom_id rather than position.' },
    { scen:3, d:4, ts:'4.3', ans:[0],
      stem:'A required, non-nullable police_report_number field produces plausible-looking numbers on claims where no police report exists. Mechanism and fix?',
      opts:['A required field demands a value, so the model fabricates one; make the field nullable so absence can be represented.',
            'The model confuses claim numbers with report numbers; add a clarifying description to the field.',
            'Temperature is too high for extraction; lower it so the model stays closer to the text.',
            'Documents exceed the context window; chunk them so the number is not guessed from truncated text.'],
      why:'A required field is an order to produce a value; when the source has none, compliance means fabrication. Nullable fields give absence a legal representation and the model uses it. The schema itself manufactured the hallucination.' },
    { scen:3, d:4, ts:'4.3', ans:[0],
      stem:'Claims arrive as motor, property or travel forms — each with its own extraction tool — and the type is unknown until the document is read. The pipeline parses tool_use blocks and must never get prose. Which tool_choice?',
      opts:['"any" — a tool call is guaranteed and the model picks the schema that fits the form.',
            'Forced selection of the motor tool, the most common type, with others as fallback.',
            '"auto" with a firm instruction to always call one of the extraction tools.',
            '"any" with parallel tool use enabled so several schemas can be tried at once.'],
      why:'"any" guarantees structured output while leaving schema choice to the model — the exact split needed when the form type is unknown. "auto" can still return prose; forcing one schema mishandles the other form types; parallel would emit several wrong extractions per document.' },
    { scen:3, d:4, ts:'4.3', ans:[0],
      stem:'Extractions pass a strict schema, yet repair_estimate_total often does not equal the sum of the itemised repairs. What does this demonstrate, and what fixes it?',
      opts:['Schemas eliminate syntax errors, not semantic ones; add validation such as a calculated_total field compared against the stated total.',
            'The schema is under-constrained; add a regex pattern to the total field so malformed totals fail validation.',
            'Strict mode is off; strict schemas verify arithmetic relationships between numeric fields.',
            'Long estimates are being truncated; raise max_tokens to recover the missing line items.'],
      why:'Shape versus truth: a total that fits the schema can still be wrong. JSON Schema cannot express cross-field arithmetic at any strictness, so semantic errors need post-extraction validation and retry feedback.' },
    { scen:3, d:4, ts:'4.4', ans:[0],
      stem:'Two extraction failures: (1) dates rendered as "12th March" instead of ISO 8601; (2) adjuster_license_no missing, though it exists only in an HR system, never in the claim document. What is true about retrying?',
      opts:['The date failure is retryable with error feedback; the licence number is not, since it is absent from the source.',
            'Both are retryable; the licence number simply needs several more attempts.',
            'Neither is retryable; both need schema changes before any retry.',
            'The licence failure is retryable because retries broaden the search; the date needs a schema change.'],
      why:'Retries fix format and structural errors — information present but rendered wrong. They cannot fix absence: each retry returns an honest null or fabricates. Ask first whether the information is actually in what was sent.' },
    { scen:3, d:4, ts:'4.3', ans:[0],
      stem:'damage_type is a closed enum, and hail, flood and vandalism claims keep landing in the nearest wrong category. Blueprint’s schema pattern?',
      opts:['Add an "other" enum value plus a detail string, and an "unclear" value for genuinely ambiguous cases.',
            'Remove the enum and accept free text, normalising values in post-processing.',
            'Expand the enum until every damage type ever observed has its own value.',
            'Return the top two candidate categories with confidence scores and take the higher.'],
      why:'Enum + "other" + detail keeps the taxonomy stable while giving oddballs an honest home; "unclear" handles ambiguity. Free text fragments into many spellings; endless expansion chases a moving target; confidence-ranked picks are the unreliable proxy.' },
    { scen:3, d:5, ts:'5.5', ans:[0],
      stem:'The claims extractor reports 96% aggregate accuracy, and finance proposes auto-approving payouts where model confidence exceeds 93%. What is the critical risk?',
      opts:['The aggregate can mask poor accuracy on specific form types or fields, and the confidence scores are uncalibrated.',
            'The model will grow overconfident as it processes more claims, requiring periodic retraining.',
            'The 93% threshold is too low for financial decisions and should be raised to 99%.',
            'Payout automation is inherently inappropriate, so every extraction must stay under human review.'],
      why:'Two unmeasured numbers: 96% overall can hide a 40% error rate on one form type, and uncalibrated confidence routes nothing. Fix the measurement — segment validation plus calibration — then automate. Blanket rejection abandons a legitimate goal; a higher uncalibrated threshold means nothing.' },
    { scen:3, d:5, ts:'5.5', ans:[0],
      stem:'After three months of calibrated 90% auto-approval, a new broker starts submitting claims as handwritten scans run through OCR — a format never seen before. The system reports 94% confidence. Appropriate safeguard?',
      opts:['Route the new-format claims to human review until enough labelled examples extend the calibration.',
            'Auto-approve, since 94% exceeds the validated threshold and the calibration has held for a quarter.',
            'Raise the global threshold to 98% for all claim types until the new format matures.',
            'Reject the submissions and ask the broker to resubmit in a supported format.'],
      why:'Calibration is valid only for the distributions in the labelled set. On an unseen format the reported 94% is uncalibrated — possibly systematically overconfident. Route novel inputs to review, which also builds the labelled sample. Raising the global threshold punishes well-calibrated formats.' },
    { scen:3, d:5, ts:'5.5', ans:[0],
      stem:'Reviewers can check 60 of 300 daily claims. Payout risk differs: total-loss claims (40/day, very high payout), glass-only claims (180/day, low payout), other damage (80/day, moderate). How should review capacity be allocated?',
      opts:['Stratify by risk: review all total-loss claims, a moderate sample of other damage, and a small sample of glass-only.',
            'Review the 60 claims where the model’s self-reported confidence is lowest.',
            'Review a uniform random 20% of every claim type for unbiased coverage.',
            'Review the first 60 claims received each day and extrapolate to the rest.'],
      why:'Allocate by consequence of error: 40 total-loss at 100%, a moderate sample of the moderate tier, a light sample of the low tier — roughly 60 reviews covering the actual risk. Raw self-reported confidence is uncalibrated; uniform sampling spends most effort where errors cost least.' },
    { scen:3, d:5, ts:'5.6', ans:[0,1],
      stem:'Two repair estimates for the same vehicle differ — ₹18,000 and ₹23,000 — and a subagent’s summary simply reported the higher figure. Which TWO practices handle this correctly? (Select 2.)',
      opts:['Annotate both figures with their sources and surface the disagreement for the coordinator to reconcile.',
            'Require estimate dates in subagents’ structured outputs, since the later figure may include a post-inspection supplement.',
            'Average the two estimates into a single compromise value.',
            'Always adopt the higher estimate to avoid under-paying the claim.',
            'Rank the repair shops by reputation and report only the higher-ranked shop’s figure.'],
      why:'Conflicts are information: preserve both values with attribution, and carry dates — apparent conflicts are often different moments (an estimate before and after a supplement). Averaging invents a number; adopting one or ranking shops is silent selection with a veneer.' },
    { scen:3, d:5, ts:'5.3', ans:[0,1],
      stem:'You are designing how the OCR subagent behaves when the OCR service fails or a page is genuinely blank. Which TWO behaviours are correct? (Select 2.)',
      opts:['Attempt local recovery for transient OCR failures, and propagate unresolvable errors to the coordinator with any partial text.',
            'Distinguish an OCR service failure from a genuinely blank page, since one is an error and the other is a valid result.',
            'Return empty text marked as success when the service fails, so downstream degrades gracefully.',
            'Halt the whole intake batch on any OCR failure, since a partial claim risks a wrong payout.',
            'Standardise all failures to one "OCR unavailable" status so the coordinator’s handling stays simple.'],
      why:'Layered, honest error propagation: recover locally, escalate the rest with context, and never conflate "the OCR broke" with "the page is blank". Empty-as-success plants a false belief downstream; halting everything over-reacts; a uniform status hides the context recovery needs.' },
    { scen:3, d:1, ts:'1.3', ans:[0],
      stem:'The coordinator hands the registry-lookup subagent exact query strings. When those return nothing, the subagent reports "insufficient results" instead of trying synonyms or alternate registries. What is the fix?',
      opts:['Give the subagent the goal and quality criteria — find the repair shop’s registration by any reliable means — and let it choose its queries.',
            'Add more exact query strings to the coordinator’s instructions to cover more cases.',
            'Have the subagent retry the same queries several times before reporting.',
            'Spawn a second subagent whose job is to check the first subagent’s results.'],
      why:'Procedural scripts freeze the adaptability that justified using an agent. State the outcome and the quality bar; the subagent can then follow leads, try alternatives and stop when the criteria are met. More exact queries just moves the wall; retrying identical queries adds nothing.' },
    { scen:3, d:1, ts:'1.3', ans:[0],
      stem:'The payout-calculation subagent ignores fraud flags the analysis subagent produced earlier, even though the coordinator received those flags. Most likely cause?',
      opts:['The coordinator did not include the fraud flags in the payout subagent’s Task prompt — subagents inherit no parent context.',
            'The payout subagent’s context window filled and evicted the flags it was given at spawn.',
            'The two subagents’ system prompts conflict, so the flags were interpreted away.',
            'The flags were too long to fit inside a single Task tool call.'],
      why:'A subagent starts fresh; the Task prompt string is the only channel from parent to child. If the coordinator did not paste the flags in, the payout agent never saw them — there was nothing to evict.' },
    { scen:3, d:1, ts:'1.2', ans:[0],
      stem:'The coordinator already holds the fully parsed claim in its context. An engineer proposes spawning a subagent to "summarise the claim" before payout is calculated. Evaluate the proposal.',
      opts:['Decline it — the coordinator has the context already; a subagent would need the claim pasted back in, adding tokens and latency for no isolation or parallelism gain.',
            'Accept it — delegating each step keeps the coordinator’s own role clean and its instructions short, which improves reliability over long sessions.',
            'Accept it — moving the summary into a subagent reduces how much of the claim the coordinator has to hold in its own context window.',
            'Accept it — subagents are more reliable than coordinators on any narrowly scoped task, so summarisation belongs there by default.'],
      why:'Subagents earn their cost through isolation, parallelism or specialisation. None applies here: the coordinator holds the context and would have to paste it into the subagent’s prompt, costing more rather than less. Delegation is a tool, not a reflex.' },
    { scen:3, d:1, ts:'1.3', ans:[0],
      stem:'Three independent checks — policy validity, prior-claims history, repair-shop registration — currently run one after another, tripling latency. How does the coordinator run them concurrently?',
      opts:['Emit all three Task tool calls within a single response.',
            'Set a parallel:true flag on each of the three AgentDefinition entries.',
            'Emit one Task call per turn across three turns and let the runtime batch them.',
            'Spawn one subagent and have it spawn the other two from within its context.'],
      why:'Concurrency comes from multiple Task tool_use blocks in one assistant turn — the parallel tool-call mechanic. parallel:true does not exist; one call per turn is accidentally sequential; nested spawning adds depth, not parallelism.' },
    { scen:3, d:1, ts:'1.5', ans:[0],
      stem:'Payouts above ₹5,00,000 must never be approved automatically. You want the approve_payout call to be impossible above that line, with such cases routed to a human. Which hook?',
      opts:['A PreToolUse hook on approve_payout that denies amounts above ₹5,00,000 and names the human-review workflow in its reason.',
            'A PostToolUse hook that reverses approvals above ₹5,00,000 after they post.',
            'A system-prompt rule in bold that large payouts require a human.',
            'A Stop hook that audits the finished session for large approvals.'],
      why:'PreToolUse can deny before execution and its reason redirects the model. PostToolUse acts after the money moved; a prompt rule is probabilistic; a Stop hook cannot block anything.' }
  ]
};
